DECISIONS & REASONING
--------------------

Decision 1: Use Railway GraphQL API instead of CLI in GitHub Actions
  - Context: Railway offers both CLI and GraphQL API for deployments
  - Rationale: CLI requires interactive login and state management
  - Evidence: Railway skill documentation shows GraphQL API patterns
  - Trade-offs: More verbose code, but more reliable in CI/CD
  - Impact: Workflow will be more stable and debuggable

Decision 2: Keep existing Vercel configuration
  - Context: Project already has vercel.json and Vercel workflows
  - Rationale: User may want to compare platforms or keep both options
  - Evidence: No explicit request to remove Vercel setup
  - Trade-offs: More configuration files, but provides flexibility
  - Impact: Project can deploy to either platform

Decision 3: Use Nixpacks builder instead of Dockerfile
  - Context: Railway supports both Nixpacks and Docker builds
  - Rationale: Nixpacks auto-detects and optimizes for Next.js
  - Evidence: Railway skill shows Nixpacks as default builder
  - Trade-offs: Less control, but simpler and faster
  - Impact: Easier maintenance, automatic optimizations

Decision 4: Create comprehensive documentation
  - Context: Railway setup requires multiple manual steps
  - Rationale: User needs clear instructions for API token, IDs, secrets
  - Evidence: Railway requires project/service/environment IDs not obvious to find
  - Trade-offs: More files to maintain, but reduces support burden
  - Impact: User can self-serve setup without back-and-forth questions

Decision 5: Use serviceInstanceDeploy(latestCommit: true)
  - Context: Railway has two deployment mutations
  - Rationale: Ensures fresh build from latest code, not cached image
  - Evidence: Railway skill shows difference between Deploy and Redeploy
  - Trade-offs: Slower than redeploy, but guarantees code updates
  - Impact: Deployments will reflect latest changes reliably

